menu "KaSe firmware"

choice KASE_DEVICE_ROLE
    prompt "Device role"
    default KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_KEYBOARD
    help
        Selects the type of device this firmware runs on.
        KEYBOARD     = standalone keyboard with local matrix (V1, V2, V2D).
        DONGLE       = USB receiver dongle for split wireless setup.
        SPLIT_SCANNER = the scanning half of a split (Niphargus right):
                        local matrix, optional screen, no keymap engine, no
                        HID output — it sends its half-matrix (see
                        KASE_SPLIT_MASTER for the other half, which reuses
                        KEYBOARD).
        MOUSE        = Conchodytes mouse: PMW3389 sensor, three clicks and a
                        wheel, mouse HID reports relayed to the dongle's slot
                        2. No matrix, no keymap, no display.

config KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_KEYBOARD
    bool "Keyboard (V1/V2/V2D)"

config KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_DONGLE
    bool "Dongle (RX from halves)"

config KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_SPLIT_SCANNER
    bool "Split scanner half (matrix + optional screen, no keymap engine — Niphargus right)"

config KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_MOUSE
    bool "Conchodytes mouse (PMW3389 sensor + clicks, HID relay to slot 2)"

endchoice

config KASE_HAS_MOUSE_SENSOR
    bool
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_MOUSE
    default n
    help
        Compiles the PMW3389 driver (main/periph/pmw3389.c) and its SROM blob.

        The blob alone is 4 KB of rodata and only makes sense on a board that
        carries the sensor — hence this symbol rather than an unconditional
        compile. It is uploaded to the chip on every boot: it's the sensor's
        firmware, not a data table.

        ⚠ The PMW3389 blob is NOT the PMW3360's (99.6% of the bytes differ).
        Swapping them means loading another chip's firmware into the sensor.

config KASE_NO_KEYMAP_ENGINE
    bool
    # Fusion: the dongle GETS BACK the keymap engine (it merges two raw
    # half-matrices and outputs the HID). This `default n` comes FIRST because
    # Kconfig keeps the first default satisfied — so it wins over the
    # `default y if DONGLE` below whenever KASE_DONGLE_FUSION is active.
    default n if KASE_DONGLE_FUSION
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_DONGLE
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_MOUSE
    default n
    help
        True for roles WITHOUT a key matrix: the dongle, which receives
        already-finished HID, and the Conchodytes mouse, which only has three
        clicks read individually. No keycodes, no layers, no per-position
        statistics, no CDC protocol KEYBOARD block.

        ⚠ THIS SYMBOL IS NEGATIVE BY DESIGN. Do not flip it into
        KASE_HAS_KEYMAP_ENGINE: a Kconfig boolean set to `n` is ABSENT from
        sdkconfig.h, exactly like on the host test harness, where no CONFIG_*
        exists at all. The preprocessor cannot tell the two apart.

        With the negative form, `#if !CONFIG_KASE_NO_KEYMAP_ENGINE` is true
        when the symbol is absent — so on the host AND on any keyboard
        target, which is the right default in both cases. With the positive
        form, the host would believe it has no keymap engine at all and
        input/key_processor.c would stop compiling. That happened on
        2026-08-25.

        It replaces the `!DONGLE && !MOUSE` list that used to be scattered
        across seven files; a third keyboard-less role only touches this
        `default`.

config KASE_HAS_LOCAL_MATRIX
    bool
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_KEYBOARD
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_SPLIT_SCANNER
    default n
    help
        Compiles matrix_scan.c and keyboard_task.c local-scan path.

        The Niphargus right half scans its own matrix: it's even its only
        function, the spec describes it as "a scanner that reports its raw
        matrix". It has, however, neither keymap engine nor HID output.

config KASE_DONGLE_FUSION
    bool "Fusion at the dongle: both halves emit RAW, engine runs at the dongle"
    default n
    help
        Re-architecture (2026-09-12 design): instead of the left merging and
        sending finished HID, both halves emit their RAW matrix to the
        dongle, which merges and runs the engine. Neither half listens on
        battery -> better battery life on both sides, and wake-up is driven
        by the dongle (end of "touch the left first").

        Two engines (dongle + left autonomous over USB) with no divergence:
        same code, config replicated and versioned in both NVS, only one
        engine active at a time depending on the routing.

        Default OFF: the current path (left as master, finished HID) stays in
        place and works as long as this flag is inactive. Phased rollout, see
        docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-12-dongle-fusion.md.

config KASE_LINK_FORCE_SOURCE
    bool "Bench: force the half to behave as a 5V SOURCE (without USB enumeration)"
    default n
    depends on KASE_LINK_WIRE
    help
        Charging diagnostic. The link only offers 5V if it believes itself
        powered, and it infers that from USB enumeration (tud_ready). But the
        right is charged by plugging the left into a wall CHARGER, which does
        not enumerate: the firmware stays blind and never closes the switch.

        This option forces "USB present" permanently: the half probes and
        closes the 5V on ACK, regardless of USB state. It lets the TRRS
        charging electrics be validated before the VBUS bridge (GPIO33, 1M
        bridge) is populated. Do NOT leave this on in production: a
        low-battery left would start powering the right.

config KASE_LINK_WIRE
    bool "Wired TRRS link: handshake and 5V (brick B2)"
    default n
    help
        UART1 transport of the inter-half link over the TRRS jack, and
        control of the LINK_5V_EN load switch via link_handshake.h's
        handshake. 5V is off by default and only closes after a verified
        exchange (probe + ACK). A half that is charging stays awake: the
        link vetoes sleep as long as its switch is closed.

config KASE_VEILLE
    bool "Hybrid sleep: light sleep then deep sleep (brick B7)"
    default n
    help
        Two stages. LIGHT: light sleep, ~240 uA, state retained, ~1 ms
        wake-up on the local matrix — cheap enough to hold for hours. DEEP:
        deep sleep, ~8 uA, EXT1 wake-up on the lines, at the cost of a full
        restart (~683 ms measured at the bench).

        ⚠ The radio is turned off as early as the light stage: listening
        costs 13.1 mA and the nRF24 has no low-power listening mode. A
        sleeping half therefore does NOT hear the other — after a long
        absence, the first keystroke must be on the half that talks to the
        host.

config KASE_VEILLE_LEGERE_S
    int "Inactivity before light sleep (seconds)"
    default 15
    depends on KASE_VEILLE
    help
        Short: the light stage retains state and wakes up in ~1 ms on the
        keystroke, which is captured (22 wake-ups out of 22 at the bench,
        2026-09-15). Awake and idle, the board draws ~28 mA at 160 MHz
        (ESP32-S3 datasheet v2.2, table 5-9, p. 67) against 0.24 mA asleep:
        every second spent waiting before sleeping costs 100 times a second
        of sleep. At 60 s, a day of typing interspersed with pauses was
        losing ~0.2 V (~13 mA average); 15 s removes three quarters of that.

config KASE_VEILLE_PROFONDE_S
    int "Inactivity before deep sleep (seconds)"
    default 14400
    depends on KASE_VEILLE
    help
        Long: 4 h by default. The light stage costs ~1 mAh over four hours,
        so there is no reason to rush toward a wake-up that restarts the
        board. Lower it temporarily (30 s) to test the EXT1 wake-up at the
        bench, otherwise it would mean waiting four hours.

config KASE_VEILLE_DIAG
    bool "Bench instrumentation of wake-up (timings, lines, re-read ladder)"
    default n
    depends on KASE_VEILLE
    help
        Bench for the "light first key lost" issue (2026-09-16..19): timings
        for entering and leaving sleep, line levels and GPIO mask at the
        first instruction after wake-up, re-read every 10 ms for 150 ms when
        the capture is empty, dump of both capture passes. Without the
        option, the wake-up path stays: capture, single re-read at 5 ms (a
        fix, not a diagnostic), driver re-creation, reconciliation. Lead
        gathered: wake-up starts, the line is already low 13 ms later, the
        key is never seen within 156 ms — contact < 13 ms.

config KASE_KEY_STATS
    bool "Typing statistics (counters, bigrams, WPM, NVS)"
    default n if KASE_SPLIT_MASTER || KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_SPLIT_SCANNER
    default y
    depends on !KASE_NO_KEYMAP_ENGINE
    help
        Per-key counters, bigrams (17 KB of RAM), WPM and their NVS save.
        Disabled on the Niphargus halves: "no stats on the keyboard, at best
        on the dongle" (2026-09-16). An NVS save stalls the instruction cache
        for the duration of a flash write — scanning and emission stop, and a
        keystroke caught in that window is lost. The stats CDC commands then
        answer with zeros.

config KASE_BATT_SENSE
    bool "Battery gauge: ADC read of VBAT_SENSE (Niphargus halves)"
    default n
    help
        Measures battery voltage every 10 s and on wake-up on
        BOARD_VBAT_SENSE_GPIO (1M/1M bridge + 100 nF, ADC2). Reported to the
        dongle in PKT_TYPE_STATUS with the half's identity; exposed via the
        BATTERY CDC. ADC2 requires no WiFi — true on the Niphargus.
        "Charging" cannot be measured directly (no VBUS): inferred from a
        plateau >= 4.15 V (full) or a rising voltage (charging likely).

config KASE_DISPLAY_MEMLCD
    bool "Sharp memory-LCD LS011B7DH03 display (Niphargus halves, portrait 68x160)"
    default n
    help
        Driver + LVGL backend for the halves' nice!view screen. The SPI bus
        is SHARED with the nRF24: every screen transaction goes through the
        radio owner's lock (rf_bus_lock). CS active-high, held low from boot.
        Image frozen in light sleep, no wake-up for the screen. Makes
        KASE_HAS_DISPLAY true (first satisfied default).

config KASE_HALF_LINK_TX
    bool "Inter-half link: emit the half-matrix (right half)"
    default n
    depends on KASE_HAS_RF_TX
    help
        Brick B3. The right half emits its half-matrix on the link channel
        (RF_CH_HALF_LINK, see rf_slot.h) on every change. It has neither
        keymap engine nor HID output: it's a scanner.

config KASE_RF_FORCE_ROUTE
    bool "Force HID over radio even when USB is plugged in"
    default n
    depends on KASE_KBD_WIRELESS
    help
        Bench diagnostic. Normally routing is USB-first: USB plugged in ->
        HID over USB, USB absent -> radio to the dongle. Testing the radio
        link therefore requires unplugging the USB, hence a working battery
        supply.

        This option forces KBD_OUT_RF permanently: USB keeps powering the
        board and carrying the CDC, but HID reports go out over radio. It
        lets the link to the dongle be validated without depending on the
        battery supply.

        The keyboard therefore no longer types over its own USB while this
        is active.

config KASE_MATRIX_LOG_CONSOLE
    bool "Log every matrix change to the console"
    default n
    depends on KASE_HAS_LOCAL_MATRIX
    help
        Bench diagnostic. Every press and every release is printed in clear
        on the console as "MTX r=<row> c=<col> press|release", bypassing the
        CDC — useful when the board's USB is unusable, or to check a pinout
        table against the hardware contract.

        Deliberately breaks the "no ESP_LOGI in matrix scan callbacks" rule
        from CLAUDE.md: it's too slow for real typing. Off by default, never
        compiled in production.

config KASE_HAS_DISPLAY
    bool
    default y if KASE_DISPLAY_MEMLCD
    default n if KASE_SPLIT_MASTER
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_KEYBOARD
    default n
    help
        Compiles display backends and status display task. Niphargus left
        (KASE_SPLIT_MASTER) has no screen at all (J12 unpopulated — see
        docs/NIPHARGUS_V2_HARDWARE.md) and must take priority over the
        KEYBOARD-role default: Kconfig honors the first satisfied `default`
        in source order, so this override has to come first.

config KASE_NO_RADIO
    bool "Strict wired keyboard: no BLE, no WiFi"
    default n
    help
      Removes the BLE stack from a keyboard used only over USB. Gain: ~500 KB
      of binary, one fewer Bluedroid task and its 2.4 GHz activity, and one
      fewer source competing with the typing path.
      Cost: the keyboard loses Bluetooth HID mode — the usb_bl_state switch
      and the Bluetooth keycodes (0x2900-0x2F00) become no-ops.

      Set in sdkconfig.defaults.<board>. This symbol has a PROMPT, so it can
      be overridden from an sdkconfig; KASE_HAS_BLE has none and can only be
      forced by the `default n if` below. Pair it with CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=n to
      also drop Bluedroid from the config.

config KASE_HAS_BLE
    bool
    # Three INDEPENDENT reasons to cut BLE, all three worth keeping:
    # KASE_NO_RADIO is a per-board choice for a strictly wired keyboard
    # (the V2D), while the two Niphargus halves cut it under a power budget
    # constraint. Do not unify under KASE_NO_RADIO: its name says "strict
    # wired keyboard", yet the Niphargus halves are wireless (nRF24). The
    # symbol would lie about what it describes.
    default n if KASE_NO_RADIO
    default n if KASE_SPLIT_MASTER
    default n if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_SPLIT_SCANNER
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_KEYBOARD && !KASE_KBD_WIRELESS
    default n
    help
        Compiles BLE HID stack (ESP-IDF Bluedroid). Disabled in wireless-relay
        mode (KASE_KBD_WIRELESS): a smart keyboard reaches the host through the
        M.2 dongle over USB, so its own BLE radio is redundant — and dropping it
        frees ~80KB heap and removes its 2.4GHz activity near the NRF relay.
        Also disabled on both Niphargus halves (KASE_SPLIT_MASTER,
        KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_SPLIT_SCANNER): the power budget (HT7833, radio peaks)
        does not allow for it — config and updates go over USB. Both `default`
        lines above must stay before the KEYBOARD-role default: Kconfig honors
        the first satisfied `default` in source order, same mechanism as
        KASE_HAS_DISPLAY above.

config KASE_KBD_WIRELESS
    bool "Keyboard talks to the M.2 dongle wirelessly (NRF HID + ESP-NOW config)"
    default n
    depends on KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_KEYBOARD
    help
      When set, a standalone keyboard relays its HID reports to the M.2 dongle
      over NRF24 and exchanges config over ESP-NOW, instead of acting as its own
      USB device. Wired keyboards (V1/V2) leave this off.

config KASE_SPLIT_MASTER
    bool "Split master half (the half with the keymap engine — Niphargus left)"
    default n
    depends on KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_KEYBOARD
    help
      Master half of a split (the Niphargus left). It reuses the KEYBOARD role (local matrix,
      keymap engine, USB HID, binary CDC) and adds what is specific to the
      split: receiving the right half's matrix (wired TRRS link, wireless
      nRF24) and emitting the final HID report to the dongle. It's the only
      half that carries a keymap engine.

config KASE_VBUS_SENSE
    bool "Use a VBUS-sense GPIO for the USB-first auto-switch"
    default n
    depends on KASE_KBD_WIRELESS
    help
      ON: read USB-cable presence from BOARD_VBUS_SENSE_GPIO (a VBUS divider) —
      reliable in both directions (plug→USB, unplug→RF). Requires the divider.
      OFF (default): fall back to tud_mounted() (USB enumerated by a host). Works
      plugged-in without any extra wiring, but unplug detection is unreliable on
      the ESP32-S3 (TinyUSB may not see the disconnect), so it can stay on USB
      after unplug. Flip ON once the GPIO33 divider is soldered.

config KASE_HAS_RF_TX
    bool
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_MOUSE
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_SPLIT_SCANNER
    default n
    help
        Compiles the nRF24 driver's TRANSMITTER mode (PTX): rf_driver_init_tx,
        rf_driver_send, and the out-of-band pairing exchange.

        This symbol used to be referenced by comm/rf/rf_driver.h without ever
        being declared — only KASE_KBD_WIRELESS opened the PTX block.
        Declaring it here gives the mouse the same access without going
        through a symbol whose name talks about a keyboard.

config KASE_HAS_RF_RX
    bool
    default y if KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_DONGLE
    default n
    help
        Compiles NRF24L01+ RX driver and rf_rx_task (dongle role).

config KASE_RF_CE_SCAN
    bool "Pairing: brute-force the NRF CE pin (bodged boards only)"
    default n
    help
        When pairing fails on the configured CE pin, tries a list of
        candidate GPIOs by driving them as output. Workaround inherited from
        the hand-bodged V2D, where the CE wire was uncertain.

        DANGEROUS on a board with a known pinout: the list
        { 47, 45, 38, 39, 40, 33, 34, 35, 36 } touches, on the Niphargus, the
        shared SPI bus (38/39/40), the trackpad's SDA (47), a forbidden
        strapping pin (45) and the N16R8's octal PSRAM (35/36 — see the
        ESP32-S3-WROOM-1 datasheet p. 12).

        The CE pin configured by board.h is in any case always tried first,
        whether this option is active or not.

config KASE_NRF_PROBE
    bool "NRF probe at boot (does the radio answer?)"
    default n
    help
        Bench diagnostic. At boot, configures the nRF24's GPIOs, opens the
        SPI bus and reads the CONFIG and RF_SETUP registers. A missing or
        badly wired module reads 0x00 or 0xFF on both, which
        rf_driver_probe() rejects.

        Installs no task and relays nothing: it is not the radio link, which
        remains the province of B3/B4 in
        docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-19-niphargus-firmware-design.md.

        Logs via ESP_LOGW (tag "rf_probe") — requires a console.

config KASE_NRF_LINE_TEST
    bool "NRF line short-circuit test at boot (bring-up diagnostic)"
    default n
    depends on KASE_HAS_RF_RX
    help
        Dongle bring-up tool. At boot, before radio init, drives each NRF line
        (SPI MOSI/MISO/SCK + per-radio CSN/CE/IRQ) and detects solder bridges
        between them. Bidirectional, so immune to a line's own idle pull-up
        (IRQ lines idle high). Logs results (ESP_LOGW tag "rf_line_test") — needs
        a console to read (pair with CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_UART_DEFAULT, since the
        dongle is CONSOLE_NONE in production). Leave off in production.

choice KASE_DONGLE_SEC_PERSONALITY
    prompt "Dongle security personality"
    depends on KASE_DEVICE_ROLE_DONGLE
    default KASE_SEC_NONE
    help
        The ESP32-S3 FS USB core has 4 data IN endpoints (ep_in_count=5
        incl. EP0). CDC (2 IN) + HID kbd (1 IN) leave exactly ONE slot,
        so the security interfaces are mutually exclusive:
        NONE    = plain keyboard dongle (CDC + HID only).
        OTP_HID = YubiKey-compatible OTP HID (CR-HMAC, KeePassXC).
        OPENPGP = CCID smartcard (gpg sign / SSH, touch-gated).

config KASE_SEC_NONE
    bool "None (plain keyboard dongle)"

config KASE_SEC_OTP_HID
    bool "YubiKey-compatible OTP HID (CR-HMAC)"

config KASE_SEC_OPENPGP
    bool "OpenPGP smartcard over CCID"

endchoice

endmenu
